<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Tubman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as William V. S. Tubman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Tubman was President of Liberia from 1944 until his death in 1971, the longest-serving president in its history. His open-door policy brought foreign investment in iron ore and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, and his unification policy extended rights to the indigenous majority, though power stayed with the Americo-Liberian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-whig-party">True Whig Party</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-tubman.jpg" alt="William Tubman" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afdrukken_ANEFO_Rousel,_fotonummer_157-1166_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Vacanarat Shadrach Tubman was born on 29 November 1895 in Harper, in south-eastern Liberia, one of five children of a poor family descended from people once enslaved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. His grandfather Alexander Tubman was a stonemason, a general in the Liberian army, a Speaker of the House of Representatives and a Methodist preacher. Alexander's parents were among 69 freed people whose passage to Liberia in 1844 was paid by their former mistress, Emily Harvie Thomas Tubman of Augusta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Tubman went to the Methodist Cape Palmas Seminary, became a lay preacher at nineteen and passed the bar in 1917 after studying law with private tutors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-whig-party">True Whig Party</a> and in 1923, aged 28, became the youngest senator in Liberian history, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland-county">Maryland County</a>. He fought for the constitutional rights of indigenous groups, which made up the overwhelming majority of Liberians. In 1931 he resigned his seat to defend Liberia before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> against allegations that the country used slave labour. After another term in the legislature, he was appointed in 1937 an associate justice of the Supreme Court by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-barclay">Edwin Barclay</a>, where he served until 1943. An official biography speculates that Barclay did so to remove him as a rival for the presidency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Tubman was elected president on 4 May 1943 and inaugurated on 3 January 1944. Liberia declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on 27 January 1944, and in June he and Barclay were the first African heads of state received at the White House. His open door policy drew foreign investment, mostly American, which grew by 200 percent between 1944 and 1970, and Liberia averaged 11.5 percent annual growth from 1950 to 1960. Roads, a railway to the iron mines, hospitals and a literacy programme followed. He also worked for national unification between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americo-liberians">Americo-Liberians</a> and the indigenous majority, and helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> of moderate states in 1961.</p><p>His rule had a harsher side. S. David Coleman and his son were hunted down and killed by soldiers for allegedly plotting against him, the constitution was changed to let him serve beyond two terms, and in 1961 he declared a state of emergency after riots and a strike. Tubman died on 23 July 1971, the longest-serving president in the country's history, and is regarded as the father of modern Liberia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Tubman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>William Tubman</b> (1944–1971) pursued an Open Door Policy towards foreign investment and a Unification Policy that extended voting rights and representation to the interior, while maintaining personal control through patronage and security services.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harper-liberia">Harper</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=4.37,-7.72&amp;year=1944" aria-label="Harper on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William Tubman"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4344 4627h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4344 4627h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=4.37,-7.72&amp;year=1944">What was here in 1944? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
